1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,450
Mini Drone Parcel Route.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:09,041
The goal is a drone that plans its own
route, reads an uncertain weather alert,

3
00:00:09,091 --> 00:00:12,170
and makes a safe choice: fly now, or wait.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:18,016
To get there, the project combines three
AI methods: A-star search, Bayesian

5
00:00:18,066 --> 00:00:21,090
inference, and a Markov decision process.

6
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:26,630
A drone needs to deliver one parcel across
a six-by-six map.

7
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:30,910
It has to get around obstacles and decide
whether to take off.

8
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:35,970
Before it flies, a weather sensor gives it
one clue about the wind.

9
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:39,590
A-star finds the shortest route.

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,870
It adds the steps taken so far to the
Manhattan distance remaining.

11
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,790
That distance counts the rows and columns
still to cross.

12
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,690
The shortest route takes ten steps.

13
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,170
A-star expands eleven nodes with this
estimate.

14
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:58,510
With the estimate set to zero, the search
expands thirty-three.

15
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,010
The route length stays the same.

16
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:08,350
Before the alert, the probability of wind
is zero point three zero.

17
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:13,321
The sensor alerts with probability zero
point eight zero when it’s windy, and zero

18
00:01:13,371 --> 00:01:15,190
point two zero when it’s calm.

19
00:01:15,660 --> 00:01:20,611
Once the alert comes in, Bayes’ rule
raises our wind belief to zero point six

20
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:21,570
three one six.

21
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:26,810
One Bellman update combines the route and
the wind belief.

22
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:31,690
Flying earns ten in calm weather and loses
twenty in wind.

23
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:37,415
Weighting those rewards by their
probabilities gives Fly a score of minus

24
00:01:37,465 --> 00:01:38,830
eight point nine five.

25
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:40,090
Wait scores minus two.

26
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:42,670
That’s higher, so the drone waits.

27
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:50,050
Now we turn off the alert.

28
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:57,557
The route stays at ten steps, but the wind
belief falls to zero point zero nine six

29
00:01:57,607 --> 00:01:57,850
eight.

30
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:01,490
That’s below the break-even probability of
zero point four zero.

31
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:02,790
The decision flips to Fly.

32
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,110
Both approaches to the goal are blocked.

33
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:12,470
A-star finds no route, so the app selects
Wait.

34
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:15,630
The drone needs a route before it can take
off.

35
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,650
All tests pass.

36
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:23,370
They cover both alert settings, the longer
route, and the blocked goal.

37
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:26,490
The two searches also agree on route
length.

38
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:33,896
What the project achieved: a working local
app, three algorithms joined into one

39
00:02:33,946 --> 00:02:36,670
decision, and ninety passing tests.

40
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,570
The limits are clear.

41
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:43,430
The numbers are invented, and the drone
looks only one step ahead.

42
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:49,130
Next steps would be full value iteration,
and tracking the wind over several alerts.
